<figure class="exposure-example" aria-label="Illustrative cash-equity positions: gross exposure is 20 million dollars and net exposure is 4 million dollars">
  <div class="example-head">
    <span class="example-label">Illustrative cash-equity positions</span>
    <strong class="example-title">The same positions. Two valid measures.</strong>
    <p>Both books hold the same issuer’s common shares, valued at the same USD closing price.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="position-ledger" role="group" aria-label="Position values in US dollars">
    <div class="position-row">
      <div class="position-name"><span class="position-mark long" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><strong>Book A</strong><small>Long position</small></span></div>
      <span class="position-value qf-figure">+$12m</span>
    </div>
    <div class="position-row">
      <div class="position-name"><span class="position-mark short" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><strong>Book B</strong><small>Short position</small></span></div>
      <span class="position-value qf-figure">−$8m</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="exposure-measures">
    <div class="measure">
      <strong class="measure-name">Gross exposure</strong>
      <span class="measure-value qf-figure">$20m</span>
      <div class="measure-bar" aria-hidden="true"><span class="gross-long"></span><span class="gross-short"></span></div>
      <span class="measure-formula qf-figure">|+$12m| + |−$8m|</span>
      <p>Add the absolute position values. Both books contribute to the total.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="measure">
      <strong class="measure-name">Net exposure</strong>
      <span class="measure-value qf-figure">$4m</span>
      <div class="measure-bar" aria-hidden="true"><span class="net-long"></span></div>
      <span class="measure-formula qf-figure">+$12m − $8m</span>
      <p>Add the signed position values. The long and short positions offset.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <span class="scale-note">Both bars use a <span class="qf-figure">$20m</span> scale. All amounts are USD.</span>
  <figcaption>Gross and net answer different questions. Net exposure describes the signed balance of these positions; it is not a measure of total risk.</figcaption>
</figure>

<style>
